    ]
  },
  {
    "alert_id": "ALERT_003",
    "title": "Ibuprofen and TDF compounding renal risk",
    "message": "The patient takes ibuprofen 400 mg 2–3 times weekly for headaches over the past 6 months. NSAIDs reduce prostaglandin-mediated renal blood flow and can impair kidney function, particularly in patients already at risk. TDF is independently nephrotoxic. The combination of TDF with regular NSAID use compounds the risk of renal injury. The patient's creatinine has risen progressively from 0.9 to 1.4 mg/dL and eGFR has fallen to 52 mL/min/1.73m², with protein 1+ on urinalysis. While TDF is the more likely primary driver of the renal decline, ibuprofen may be accelerating or exacerbating the injury. Discontinuing or reducing ibuprofen is a modifiable risk factor.",
    "issue_type": "drug_interaction",
    "severity": "moderate",
    "recommended_action": "Counsel patient to discontinue or minimise ibuprofen use. Explore alternative headache management strategies. Monitor renal function closely if NSAID use cannot be avoided.",
